id: pm-web-z1vm
title: "The catalog completeness gate cannot fail in CI, and a package it should have caught has been missing since it was created"
description: "pm-web carries a test whose stated purpose is the completeness direction that the other catalog tests do not cover: every pm extension present in the fleet must appear in the catalog, so a package added to the fleet cannot be silently missing from the UI. It derives the expected set from the fleet directory rather than restating it as a second list, which is the right design and is explicitly explained in the test.\n\nIt cannot fire where it matters. The test opens with an early return when the fleet siblings are not resolvable, and in CI pm-web is checked out standalone with no sibling directories, so the derived set is empty and the assertion is skipped. The gate therefore passes vacuously on every pull request and only ever runs on a developer machine that happens to have the whole fleet cloned beside it. A gate that cannot fail in the environment that guards the merge is not a gate.\n\nIt was measured, not theorised: running the suite locally with the fleet present fails with actual ['pm-ado'] against expected [], and pm-ado has been in the fleet with a manifest.json declaring five capabilities since it was created. Every pm-web CI run over that period was green.\n\nThere is a second, independent problem in the same area. The sibling availability test decides whether a package is publishable by asking whether its package.json declares publishConfig.access. That is a convention rather than a measurement, and pm-ado already breaks it: it declares publishConfig.access public while being release-gated behind vars.PM_RELEASE_APPROVED and absent from npm, so cataloguing it by that rule would render an install button for a package npm answers 404 for — the exact outcome the assertion's own message says it exists to prevent."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[2]: "area:catalog",gates
created_at: "2026-09-03T17:25:58.162Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-03T18:23:00.329Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-03T18:23:00.299Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-03T18:23:00.299Z"
author: codex
acceptance_criteria: "pm-ado appears in the catalog with an availability that matches its real installability; the completeness assertion fails a CI run when a fleet package is absent from the catalog, demonstrated by a deliberate omission on a branch rather than argued; the availability rule is decided by something that measures whether publication can happen rather than by the presence of a package.json convention; and no gate in this area retains an early return that turns absence of input into a pass"
risk: medium
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-03T18:22:05.489Z",codex,"Measured the blindness rather than reasoning about it, and it is worse than the item first recorded: two gates degrade to tautologies in a CI checkout, not one.\n\nThe completeness assertion returns early when the fleet siblings are unresolvable, which is every CI run. The membership assertion is worse in kind: EXPECTED_NAMES is defined as FLEET_EXTENSIONS when siblings resolve and catalogNames() otherwise, so in CI it compares the catalog against itself and can only ever pass. Both were green throughout the period pm-ado sat in the fleet with a manifest declaring five capabilities and no catalog entry.\n\nThe fix gives the gate an input that exists in CI. A committed snapshot at test/fleet-extensions.snapshot.json records every fleet extension with its manifest description, its package.json description, its sorted capabilities and whether it declares publishConfig; a new assertion reads it and fails when the catalog omits an entry, misdescribes one, or declares an availability the package contradicts. The snapshot is an input and never the source of truth: a companion assertion re-derives it from the live fleet wherever the siblings resolve, so a stale snapshot fails loudly instead of quietly narrowing what CI checks, and the generator refuses to write an empty snapshot because an empty one would restore exactly the vacuous pass it was written to remove.\n\nProved the new gate fires by deleting the pm-ado entry from the catalog and running the suite: it fails with the missing package named. A gate that cannot be shown to fail is indistinguishable from one that cannot fail.\n\npm-ado is now catalogued as unreleased, which required a change in pm-ado itself: it declared publishConfig.access while being release-gated and absent from npm, so cataloguing it by the existing rule would have rendered an install button for a package npm answers 404 for. That is tracked as pm-ado-tg1f and shipped separately.\n\nThe derivation module is covered at 100 percent lines, branches and functions against a synthetic tree rather than the real siblings, because every real package is well formed and reading them would only ever exercise the happy path."
tests[2]{command,path,scope,timeout_seconds,provenance{author,created_at,source_kind,source_ref},note}:
  "npx tsx --test --test-name-pattern \"the catalog covers every fleet extension in the committed snapshot\" test/catalog.test.ts",test/catalog.test.ts,project,180,codex,"2026-09-03T18:22:06.500Z",local_mutation,fix/close-the-qs-advisory-and-catalog-the-azure-devops-package,the CI-capable completeness gate; proved to fail by deleting the pm-ado catalog entry
  npx tsx --test test/fleet-snapshot.test.ts,test/fleet-snapshot.test.ts,project,120,codex,"2026-09-03T18:22:06.500Z",local_mutation,fix/close-the-qs-advisory-and-catalog-the-azure-devops-package,"snapshot derivation, 100 percent branches against a synthetic fleet"
docs[1]{path,scope,note}:
  "https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/blob/main/.agents/pm/issues/pm-ado-tg1f.toon",project,the sibling change this entry depends on
close_reason: "The completeness gate now reads a committed fleet snapshot, so it has input in a standalone CI checkout and fails there when the catalog drifts. Proved to fire by deleting the pm-ado entry. A companion assertion re-derives the snapshot from the live fleet so it cannot go stale unnoticed, and pm-ado is catalogued with an availability matching its real installability."
body: ""
